# Must all inner-product preserving maps be linear?

**Answer: Yes.** Every inner-product preserving map between inner-product spaces is necessarily linear (and injective). Below is a complete proof working over $\mathbb{C}$ (the real case follows by the same argument with all conjugations dropped).

---

## Setup

Let $(V, \langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_V)$ and $(W, \langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_W)$ be inner-product spaces over $\mathbb{F}\in\{\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C}\}$, and let $f:V\to W$ satisfy

$$\langle f(x), f(y)\rangle_W = \langle x, y\rangle_V \qquad \text{for all } x,y\in V. \tag{IP}$$

We prove $f$ is $\mathbb{F}$-linear.

## Step 1 — $f$ preserves norms

Setting $y=x$ in (IP):

$$\|f(x)\|_W^2 = \langle f(x),f(x)\rangle_W = \langle x,x\rangle_V = \|x\|_V^2,$$

so $\|f(x)\|_W = \|x\|_V$ for every $x\in V$. In particular $f(0)=0$.

## Step 2 — Additivity

Fix $x,y\in V$ and set $z = x+y$. We show $f(z)=f(x)+f(y)$ by proving the squared norm of their difference is zero:

$$\bigl\|f(z)-f(x)-f(y)\bigr\|_W^2.$$

Expanding using conjugate-linearity in the first slot / linearity in the second (complex convention), and applying (IP) to every pair $\langle f(a),f(b)\rangle = \langle a,b\rangle$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\bigl\|f(z)-f(x)-f(y)\bigr\|^2
&= \langle f(z)-f(x)-f(y),\, f(z)-f(x)-f(y)\rangle_W \\
&= \|f(z)\|^2 - \langle f(z),f(x)\rangle - \langle f(z),f(y)\rangle \\
&\quad - \langle f(x),f(z)\rangle + \|f(x)\|^2 + \langle f(x),f(y)\rangle \\
&\quad - \langle f(y),f(z)\rangle + \langle f(y),f(x)\rangle + \|f(y)\|^2 \\[2pt]
&= \|z\|^2 - \langle z,x\rangle - \langle z,y\rangle - \langle x,z\rangle + \|x\|^2 + \langle x,y\rangle \\
&\quad - \langle y,z\rangle + \langle y,x\rangle + \|y\|^2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Now substitute $z = x+y$ and use $\langle z,x\rangle = \langle x,x\rangle + \langle y,x\rangle$, $\langle z,y\rangle = \langle x,y\rangle + \langle y,y\rangle$, $\langle x,z\rangle = \langle x,x\rangle + \langle x,y\rangle$, $\langle y,z\rangle = \langle y,x\rangle + \langle y,y\rangle$, and $\|z\|^2 = \|x\|^2 + \langle x,y\rangle + \langle y,x\rangle + \|y\|^2$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\bigl[\|x\|^2 + \langle x,y\rangle + \langle y,x\rangle + \|y\|^2\bigr] \\
&-\bigl[\|x\|^2 + \langle y,x\rangle\bigr] -\bigl[\langle x,y\rangle + \|y\|^2\bigr] \\
&-\bigl[\|x\|^2 + \langle x,y\rangle\bigr] + \|x\|^2 + \langle x,y\rangle \\
&-\bigl[\langle y,x\rangle + \|y\|^2\bigr] + \langle y,x\rangle + \|y\|^2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Collecting coefficients of each of the four distinct terms $\|x\|^2,\|y\|^2,\langle x,y\rangle,\langle y,x\rangle$:

| term | coefficients | sum |
|---|---|---|
| $\|x\|^2$ | $+1-1-1+1$ | $0$ |
| $\|y\|^2$ | $+1-1-1+1$ | $0$ |
| $\langle x,y\rangle$ | $+1-1-1+1$ | $0$ |
| $\langle y,x\rangle$ | $+1-1-1+1$ | $0$ |

Hence $\bigl\|f(x+y)-f(x)-f(y)\bigr\|^2 = 0$, and by positive-definiteness of the norm,

$$f(x+y) = f(x) + f(y). \qquad \checkmark$$

## Step 3 — Homogeneity

Fix $x\in V$, $\alpha\in\mathbb{F}$, set $z = \alpha x$. We show $\|f(z) - \alpha f(x)\|^2 = 0$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\bigl\|f(z) - \alpha f(x)\bigr\|^2
&= \|f(z)\|^2 - \bar\alpha\,\langle f(z),f(x)\rangle - \alpha\,\langle f(x),f(z)\rangle + |\alpha|^2\|f(x)\|^2 \\
&= \|z\|^2 - \bar\alpha\,\langle z,x\rangle - \alpha\,\langle x,z\rangle + |\alpha|^2\|x\|^2.
\end{aligned}
$$

With $z = \alpha x$: $\|z\|^2 = |\alpha|^2\|x\|^2$, $\langle z,x\rangle = \alpha\|x\|^2$, $\langle x,z\rangle = \bar\alpha\|x\|^2$. Substituting:

$$|\alpha|^2\|x\|^2 - \bar\alpha\cdot\alpha\|x\|^2 - \alpha\cdot\bar\alpha\|x\|^2 + |\alpha|^2\|x\|^2 = (1-1-1+1)|\alpha|^2\|x\|^2 = 0.$$

So $\|f(\alpha x) - \alpha f(x)\|^2 = 0$, giving

$$f(\alpha x) = \alpha\, f(x). \qquad \checkmark$$

## Conclusion

Steps 2 and 3 together show $f$ is additive and homogeneous, hence $\mathbb{F}$-linear. (Injectivity is immediate: $f(x)=0 \Rightarrow \|x\|=\|f(x)\|=0 \Rightarrow x=0$.)

Therefore **every inner-product preserving map is linear**. $\blacksquare$

### PROOF COMPLETE
